    each differs from its LR mirror by exactly 2 cells (1 pixel displaced) [observed]
    no UD or rot180 symmetry [observed]
  total_foreground: 12-18% of grid area [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (symmetric mask first, then break symmetry for others)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid H in [21,22], W in [16,20], N_colors in [4,5], bg=0
    2. Generate the symmetric mask -- build a random sparse pattern on a crop
       of odd width (5-9) and height (3-6), fill ~40-55%, enforce LR symmetry
       by mirroring left half to right half. Ensure NOT UD-symmetric.
    3. Generate N-1 asymmetric masks -- for each, build a crop (2-8 x 4-9),
       start with a random sparse pattern, then verify it is NOT LR-symmetric.
       Easiest method -- build an LR-symmetric pattern then displace exactly
       1 pixel to break symmetry (move one colored cell to its mirror-vacant
       position's neighbor), guaranteeing diff==2.
    4. Assign distinct non-bg colors to all N masks.
    5. Place all masks on the grid with non-overlapping bboxes and gap >= 1.
       Use greedy placement or rejection sampling on positions.
    6. Validate -- exactly 1 LR-symmetric crop among all colors.
